<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alauddin Husain Shah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1519">1519</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Husain Shah</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alauddin Husain Shah was sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> from 1494 to 1519 and founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husain-shahi-dynasty">Husain Shahi dynasty</a>. He restored order after a period of palace coups, conquered Kamatapur around 1498, campaigned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>, and patronised Bengali literature. His reign is often regarded as a high point of the Bengal Sultanate.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alauddin-husain-shah.jpg" alt="Alauddin Husain Shah" width="320" height="163" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AlbeitPK · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_Alauddin_Husain_Shah_(1493-1519).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>His origins are unclear, though he is widely held to have been of Sayyid Arab descent. The Riyaz-us-Salatin says his father, Sayyid Ashraf al-Husaini, lived long in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/termez">Termez</a> before settling at Chandpur in Rarh, where Husain studied under the local qadi and married his daughter; other traditions place him in Rangpur, and Nitish Sengupta stresses his Bengali mother. He rose to be <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wazir">wazir</a> of the Abyssinian sultan Shamsuddin Muzaffar Shah. Openly leading the rebels against him, he besieged the citadel, and according to the historian Nizamuddin had the sultan secretly killed with the help of the paiks, the palace guards.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He executed twelve thousand soldiers who kept plundering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaur">Gaur</a>, disbanded the paiks and replaced the Habshis in office with Bengalis, Turks and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikandar-lodi">Sikandar Lodi</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> in 1496, the two sultans agreed on a border at Barh near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patna">Patna</a>. His general Shah Ismail Ghazi took the Kamata capital, imprisoned King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nilambar">Nilambar</a> and annexed the land up to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajo">Hajo</a>, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guwahati">Guwahati</a>. Ismail's raid into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a> in 1508-09 was driven back by the Gajapati king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prataparudra-deva">Prataparudra Deva</a>, but border fighting continued and by 1515 Odisha was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of Bengal.</p><p>His forces also extended the sultanate into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sylhet">Sylhet</a> and Kachar, fought four times in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripura">Tripura</a>, and in 1516 an expedition under his son Nasrat made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mrauk-u">Mrauk U</a> recognise Bengali rule over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chittagong">Chittagong</a> and northern Arakan. The Portuguese official Tome Pires called him a warrior who prevailed over greater kings. Under his governor Paragal Khan, Kabindra Parameshvar wrote a Bengali adaptation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a>, and Bijay Gupta wrote his Manasamangal Kavya in his reign. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> subjects called him Nripati Tilak, and he ordered his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qazi">qazis</a> to leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaitanya-mahaprabhu">Chaitanya Mahaprabhu</a> unharmed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He died in 1519 and was succeeded by his eldest surviving son, Nasrat Shah, crown prince since 1515; he was possibly buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaur">Gaur</a> near the Qadam Rasul Mosque, though his black stone tomb has disappeared. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>'s trade flourished in his reign, and Ludovico de Varthema recorded fifty ships a year loading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> at Gaur. The historian R. C. Majumdar compared him with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>, while also noting that he destroyed some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temples during his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a> campaigns.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alauddin Husain Shah” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Alauddin Husain Shah</b> (r. 1494–1519) conquered the kingdom of Kamata in 1498 and campaigned in Orissa, Tripura and the Brahmaputra valley, though attempts to subdue the Ahom kingdom failed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bengal-sultanate">Bengal Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Husain Shah</b> took refuge in Bengal, and Jaunpur became a province of the Lodi sultanate and later the Mughal Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/jaunpur-sultanate">Jaunpur Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1498 <b>Alauddin Husain Shah</b> of the Bengal Sultanate captured Kamatapur by a ruse and ended Khen rule.</blockquote><a href="/state/kamata-kingdom">Kamata Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
